<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Province of Guayaquil</h1><p class="st-meta">1820 CE – 1822 CE · capital Guayaquil</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The independent port province of Guayaquil, freed in 1820, whose fate was settled when Bolívar annexed it on the eve of his meeting with San Martín.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 9 October 1820 a group of patriots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a>, the main port of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-audiencia-of-quito">Royal Audiencia of Quito</a>, overthrew the Spanish authorities. A cabildo declared the Free Province of Guayaquil, with the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-joaquin-de-olmedo">José Joaquín de Olmedo</a> as head of government.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province adopted a provisional regulation of government and created an army, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protective-division-of-quito">Protective Division of Quito</a>, to free the highlands. Its first campaigns failed, but with troops sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a>, and Peruvian reinforcements, the patriots won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pichincha">Pichincha</a> in May 1822. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil's</a> elite was divided over joining <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> or staying independent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> in July 1822 and annexed it to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-joaquin-de-olmedo">Olmedo</a> left in protest. Days later Bolívar met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">San Martín</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil-conference">Guayaquil Conference</a>. Guayaquil became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-ecuador">Republic of Ecuador</a> in 1830.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-province-of-guayaquil.jpg" alt="Free Province of Guayaquil" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Sageo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_la_Provincia_Libre_de_Guayaquil.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1820 CE – 1822 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guayaquil</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José Joaquín de Olmedo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ecuador</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Provincia Libre de Guayaquil</dd></dl></aside></div>
